Method signature: public Object set(int index, Object element)

Implementation: if (!tainted) tainted = isTainted(element);
if (typeSafe) checkTypeSafe(element);
return super.set(index, element);

Comment: "If the index is non-negative and less than the initial size of the list, then the result is the same as the old value at that index."

We need to express: If 0 <= index && index < old size, then the returned value (\result) equals the old element at that index (\old(this.get(index)))? Actually super.set returns previous element at that index. Since we are overriding set, we need to assert that the result equals old value at that index, assuming index within bounds. Use this.get? The class extends ArrayList<Object>. The observer method get returns RoleUnresolved, but here set works on Object. But we can still use this.get(index) returns RoleUnresolved, which is a subclass of Object. For old value, we need \old(this.get(index)). Since get returns RoleUnresolved, but result is Object; okay.

Thus assert: (0 <= index && index < \old(this.size())) => \result.equals(\old(this.get(index)))? Actually equality for objects: use ==? In spec examples they used .equals for query. Here we may use == or .equals. Since get returns RoleUnresolved, we can compare with ==? Better use .equals if not null. But spec often uses .equals for objects. Use \result == \old(this.get(index))? Not sure. Use .equals.

Thus: assert (0 <= index && index < \old(this.size())) => \result.equals(\old(this.get(index)));

But need parentheses: assert (0 <= index && index < \old(this.size())) => \result.equals(\old(this.get(index)));

Return type Object, .equals method exists.

Thus final answer.assistantfinal<code>assert (0 <= index && index < \old(this.size())) => \result.equals(\old(this.get(index)));</code>
