analysisWe need to produce assert statement for comment after set method.

Method:

@Override
public Object set(int index, Object element) {
    if (!tainted)
        tainted = isTainted(element);
    if (typeSafe)
        checkTypeSafe(element);
    return super.set(index, element);
    // @@@ If the index is non-negative and less than the initial size of the list, then the result is the same as the old value at that index.
}

We need to write assert that captures: if index >=0 && index < old size then the returned result equals old value at that index. Need to refer to old size: \old(this.size()) maybe. Also need old value: \old(this.get(index))? But get returns RoleUnresolved. However set returns Object (the old element). Since the list extends ArrayList<Object>, super.set returns Object old element. So we assert: (index >=0 && index < \old(this.size())) => \result.equals(\old(this.get(index)))? Actually \result is Object, old value is Object. Use ==? Should use .equals maybe, but typical spec uses == for reference equality? In earlier examples they used this.index_of(value) == ... They used equality operator for ints, and .equals for objects. For object equality they'd use .equals. In earlier example they used this.query(i).equals(\old(this.query(i))) . So for result we can use \result.equals(\old(this.get(index))) . However need to ensure \old(this.get(index)) is allowed because get may be defined for RoleUnresolved; but we can still call it. Use \old(this.get(index)). Since get returns RoleUnresolved, which is a subclass of Object, ok.

Thus assert: assert (index >= 0 && index < \old(this.size())) => \result.equals(\old(this.get(index)));

But need parentheses around implication: (index>=0 && index<\old(this.size())) => \result.equals(\old(this.get(index)));

Return type is Object, .equals method exists. If result could be null? But we assume not.

Thus final answer.

